+---
+id: dl-001-json-pointer-single-jsonpointererror-strict-rfc-6901-array-index-grammar
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Pointer: single JsonPointerError, strict RFC 6901 array-index grammar"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` must throw "when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it
+does not resolve to a value of the document" (two distinct failure causes). RFC 6901 also leaves a few
+edge cases that need an explicit call: the `-` array token, leading-zero array indices, and malformed
+`~` escapes.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Both failure causes throw the same exported `JsonPointerError` class, rather than two separate error
+  types. Nothing downstream needs to distinguish "bad syntax" from "no such value" differently today;
+  adding two classes now would be speculative. This can be split later if a consumer needs to tell them
+  apart (e.g. to return a 400 vs. a 404).
+- Array-index tokens are validated against the RFC's exact grammar (`0`, or a non-zero digit followed by
+  more digits). A leading-zero index (`/a/01`) or the `-` token (RFC 6901's "member after the last
+  element") both fail resolution, since this function only reads a document and `-` and `01` never
+  identify an existing value in one.
+- `~` escapes are validated strictly: a `~` not immediately followed by `0` or `1` is a syntax error, not
+  treated as a literal `~`. This follows the RFC's reference-token grammar instead of being lenient.
+- `pointer`'s type is checked at runtime, not left to the TypeScript signature alone, because other
+  services (not necessarily TypeScript) call into this library.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers that need to distinguish "malformed pointer" from "pointer not found" must inspect the error
+  message, since `JsonPointerError` doesn't carry a discriminant. Acceptable for now; revisit if a real
+  caller needs it.
+- `resolvePointer` never silently returns `undefined` for a missing path; every miss is an exception.
+  Future features that build on this (e.g. an optional/"get or default" variant) should wrap this
+  function rather than change its throwing behaviour.
