+---
+id: task-001-implement-json-pointer-rfc-6901-resolvepointer
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) resolvePointer"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `resolvePointer(document, pointer)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing RFC 6901 JSON
+Pointer resolution:
+
+- Parses the pointer string into reference tokens, unescaping `~1` → `/` then `~0` → `~` in that order,
+  as the RFC prescribes to avoid ambiguity.
+- Walks the document token by token: object member lookup by exact key, or array index lookup following
+  the RFC's array-index grammar (`0`, or a non-zero digit followed by digits; no leading zeros, no `-`).
+- Throws `JsonPointerError` (exported alongside `resolvePointer`) in two situations: the pointer string
+  itself is not syntactically valid (doesn't start with `/` and isn't empty, or has a malformed `~`
+  escape), or the pointer is syntactically valid but does not resolve to a value in the given document
+  (missing member, out-of-bounds/invalid array index, or descending into a scalar).
+- `pointer`'s type is checked at runtime (not just via the TypeScript signature), since the library is
+  consumed by other services and may receive non-TS callers.
+
+Test-first: wrote `src/index.test.ts` against `node:test`/`node:assert` before the implementation
+existed (confirmed the suite failed for the right reason — missing module), then implemented
+`src/index.ts` until all 11 cases passed. Tests cover the full RFC 6901 Appendix B example document, a
+nested object/array pointer, the empty-pointer case, and the error paths listed above (invalid syntax,
+missing member, out-of-bounds index, leading-zero index, the `-` array token, non-string pointer
+argument, descending into a scalar).
+
+Verified `src/index.ts` type-checks cleanly under the project's strict `tsconfig.json` using a temporary
+`typescript` install (the repo has no `typescript`/`@types/node` devDependencies or build/typecheck
+script yet, so this was a one-off check, not wired into CI).
+
+Documented the new API in `README.md` under a new "API" section, per the documentation directive.
+
+## Follow-ups (not done here, out of scope for this task)
+
+- No `typescript`/`@types/node` devDependencies or `typecheck` script exist in the repo. `node --test`
+  runs `.test.ts` files directly via Node's native TypeScript type-stripping, which requires intra-repo
+  relative imports to use the real `.ts` extension (confirmed experimentally) — this is what
+  `src/index.test.ts` does. If a build/typecheck step is added later, this will need reconciling with
+  the `tsc`/NodeNext convention of writing `.js` extensions in relative imports.
